{
    "title": "Reef Fish Fishery of the Gulf of America; Reopening of the Red Snapper Recreational For-Hire Fishing Season in the Gulf of America",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 12, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-12/pdf/2025-22706.pdf",
    "summary": "Big changes in federal rules this week: charter boats in the Gulf can once again take customers red snapper fishing for the rest of 2025, and a new protected area off California will keep heavy fishing gear away from a deep\u2011sea coral research site. Separately, the Federal Communications Commission cleaned up several wireless rule sections by removing and reserving them.\n\nFor anglers and coastal businesses in the Gulf, the reopening matters right away. The National Marine Fisheries Service says charter and headboat trips have not caught their full share of red snapper this year, so the season is reopening from December 11 through the first minute of January 1. That gives captains a few more weeks to run trips, support crews, and bring visitors to marinas, hotels, and restaurants during the holidays. Private recreational anglers are on a different schedule, so this change mainly helps those fishing on federally permitted charter and headboat trips. Some conservation voices may worry about added pressure late in the year, but the agency says the extra days still keep harvest within the limits set to protect the stock.\n\nOn the West Coast, the government finalized a closure to bottom\u2011contact groundfish gear at Sur Ridge inside Monterey Bay National Marine Sanctuary, starting January 12, 2026. This creates space for coral research and restoration without the risk of heavy gear disturbing fragile habitat. Scientists and restoration teams gain a safer, more predictable setting to grow and study deep\u2011sea corals, which can benefit marine life over time. Commercial groundfish vessels that use gear touching the seafloor will lose access to that specific area, which may mean longer runs or shifts in where they fish.\n\nLastly, the communications agency removed and reserved a number of older rule sections in its wireless services. This kind of housekeeping can make the rulebook clearer and easier to navigate. Some operators may need to review internal practices to align with the updated structure, but the changes appear administrative rather than a major policy shift.\n\nOverall, these moves aim to balance short\u2011term access with long\u2011term stewardship and cleaner rulebooks, with varied impacts depending on where you work and fish.",
    "embedding": [
